This finite independent probe uses the frozen selection-correction Vault client/server and a synthetic encrypted in-memory store. It renders a Vault view, detaches it as shared-host navigation does, renders a newer Vault view and completes its requests, then invokes only the older client's still-scheduled logical five-minute callback. The active session changes from unlocked to locked. No real five-minute wait, performance measurement, current HTTP loss, or original GUI execution is claimed.

The shared host invokes any cleanup returned by a client before replacing its view. The final owned harness models that call and only fires an old callback if its timer remains scheduled. The frozen Vault client returns no cleanup. Its timer checks whether its content remains a child of its own root; that remains true after the root itself detaches. The retained pre-history production client has the same timer guard and also returns no cleanup, so this is a pre-existing lifecycle defect rather than a history algorithm regression.

The first harness failed before exercising the effect because it counted both the client's and the server's five-minute timers. Exact inputs, raw output and observations remain under `failures/timer-selector-v1`. The second version selects the actual async client callback and reproduces the lock; its exact source/output remain under `failures/idle-repro-v2`. The final host-cleanup adapter produces the same lock against the unchanged client and is preserved under `runs/host-contract`; none of the earlier version records were rewritten.

No author stage, production file, earlier review, or published results were changed. This finding requires a separately authenticated correction. It is a finite lifecycle safety review, not a full parity or security assessment.
